<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaibara Ekken</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1630">1630</a>–<a href="/years/1714">1714</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaibara Ekken was a Japanese Neo-Confucian scholar and botanist who served the Kuroda lords of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> domain. He wrote widely in plain Japanese on ethics, education, health and natural history, including the Yamato honzō, a study of Japanese plants and animals, and the Yōjōkun, a guide to healthy living. Works on the conduct of women attributed to him were widely read in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaibara-ekken.jpg" alt="Kaibara Ekken" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WolfgangMichel · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaibara_Ekiken_monument.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kaibara Ekken, also read Ekiken and known as Atsunobu, was born on 17 December 1630 into a family of advisers to the daimyō of Fukuoka Domain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikuzen-province">Chikuzen Province</a>. He went with his father to Edo in 1648 and in 1649 was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> to study Western science. At his father's urging he continued his studies there as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> from 1650 to 1656, then re-entered the service of the Kuroda, which allowed him to study further in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. After his father's death in 1665 he returned to Fukuoka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His two main contributions were a study of nature that blended Western natural science with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>, and the rendering of the complex writings of Neo-Confucianism into vernacular Japanese. His science kept to botany and materia medica and was concerned with natural law; the Yamato honzō of 1709 was a seminal study of Japanese plants, and the German Japanologist Philipp Franz von Siebold later called him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>As an educator he turned the ethics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> into easy manuals of conduct, among them Precepts for Children and the Yōjōkun of 1713. He also wrote on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>, in the Jingikun and a treatise on the non-divergence of Shintō and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, as well as a history of Dazaifu Shrine. His synthesis of Confucian ideas and Western science influenced the formation of Shinto, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-shinto">State Shinto</a>, and shared concerns with the Kokugaku movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kaibara died on 5 October 1714, and his Taigiroku, The Record of Great Doubts, appeared after his death the same year. The Greater Learning for Women (Onna daigaku), long his best-known book, is argued by modern scholarship to have been prepared by other hands, although the oldest surviving copy, of 1733, ends with the words as related by our teacher Ekiken Kaibara, and its colophon says it was written from his lectures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaibara Ekken” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…wives were expected to obey the head of the house, took no part in politics and rarely headed a household, though a widow could become its senior member; the Greater Learning for Women (Onna daigaku), attributed to <b>Kaibara Ekken</b>, told a wife to obey her husband in everything.</blockquote><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Social Hierarchy of Tokugawa Japan</a></li><li><blockquote>The Confucian scholar <b>Kaibara Ekken</b> served the domain, and Kuroda Nagahiro promoted Western learning in the 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/fukuoka-domain">Fukuoka Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikuzen-province">Chikuzen Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikuzen-province">Chikuzen Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.6,130.49&amp;year=1684" aria-label="Chikuzen Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7882 945 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kaibara Ekken"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8332 1215h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8332 1215h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.6,130.49&amp;year=1684">What was here in 1684? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
